From: crackerjacques <jack@supremeoverlordjabs.co>
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: sprd: resume the DMA channel instead of re-submitting

sprd_pcm_trigger() folds PAUSE_RELEASE and RESUME in with START and
answers all three by submitting a fresh descriptor and issuing pending.
That works for START and cannot work for the other two.

dmaengine_pause() sets SPRD_DMA_CHN_PAUSE and leaves schan->cur_desc
alone. The only place that clears that register again is
sprd_dma_start(), and sprd_dma_issue_pending() calls it only while
cur_desc is NULL. So after a pause the channel is never restarted: the
submit merely queues a second descriptor behind the paused one and the
hardware stays stopped for the rest of the stream's life. There is no
dmaengine_resume() anywhere in this driver.

What that costs on RG Rotate is not a missing pause button. This SKU
plays through the DSP scene, where the DSP owns the read side of the
MCDT fifo and keeps draining it on its own. Pause the AP side and the
fifo starves while the DSP runs on, so the pause is audible as a
sustained buzz rather than silence, and the resume never arrives.

The in-tree driver for the same controller, sound/soc/sprd/sprd-pcm-dma.c,
already splits the four cases the way the dmaengine API intends and calls
dmaengine_resume() here. Do the same.

Signed-off-by: crackerjacques <jack@supremeoverlordjabs.co>
---
diff --git a/sound/soc/sprd/vendor/sprd-dmaengine-pcm.c b/sound/soc/sprd/vendor/sprd-dmaengine-pcm.c
index 2bce85075ae1..54543999b4f3 100755
--- a/sound/soc/sprd/vendor/sprd-dmaengine-pcm.c
+++ b/sound/soc/sprd/vendor/sprd-dmaengine-pcm.c
@@ -1561,8 +1561,6 @@ static int sprd_pcm_trigger(struct snd_pcm_substream *substream, int cmd)
 	 */
 	switch (cmd) {
 	case SNDRV_PCM_TRIGGER_START:
-	case SNDRV_PCM_TRIGGER_RESUME:
-	case SNDRV_PCM_TRIGGER_PAUSE_RELEASE:
 		normal_dma_protect_spin_lock(substream);
 		if (rtd->dma_tx_des[0] == NULL) {
 			normal_dma_protect_spin_unlock(substream);
@@ -1584,6 +1582,32 @@ static int sprd_pcm_trigger(struct snd_pcm_substream *substream, int cmd)
 		pr_info("pcm Start\n");
 		normal_dma_protect_spin_unlock(substream);
 		break;
+	case SNDRV_PCM_TRIGGER_RESUME:
+	case SNDRV_PCM_TRIGGER_PAUSE_RELEASE:
+		/*
+		 * PAUSE_PUSH and SUSPEND leave the hardware pause bit set with
+		 * the descriptor still loaded, so there is nothing to submit
+		 * here. Only sprd_dma_start() rewrites SPRD_DMA_CHN_PAUSE, and
+		 * sprd_dma_issue_pending() skips it while cur_desc is still
+		 * set - re-submitting therefore queues a second descriptor and
+		 * leaves the channel paused for good. Lift the pause instead,
+		 * which is what the in-tree sprd-pcm-dma.c does for the same
+		 * controller.
+		 */
+		normal_dma_protect_spin_lock(substream);
+		if (rtd->dma_chn[0] == NULL) {
+			normal_dma_protect_spin_unlock(substream);
+			pr_info("%s dma_chn[0] is null ret=%#x, %d\n",
+				__func__, -ENODATA, -ENODATA);
+			return -ENODATA;
+		}
+		for (i = 0; i < rtd->hw_chan; i++) {
+			if (rtd->dma_chn[i])
+				dmaengine_resume(rtd->dma_chn[i]);
+		}
+		pr_info("pcm Resume\n");
+		normal_dma_protect_spin_unlock(substream);
+		break;
 	case SNDRV_PCM_TRIGGER_STOP:
 	case SNDRV_PCM_TRIGGER_SUSPEND:
 	case SNDRV_PCM_TRIGGER_PAUSE_PUSH:
